<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhaskara II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1114">1114</a>–<a href="/years/1185">1185</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Bhaskaracharya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bhaskara II was an Indian mathematician and astronomer, author of the Siddhanta Shiromani, whose parts include the Lilavati on arithmetic and the Bijaganita on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>. His work marks a high point of classical Indian mathematics. In 1207 his grandson Changadeva founded a school at Patan to teach his works, under the patronage of the Seuna <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> king Singhana.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhaskara-ii.jpg" alt="Bhaskara II" width="300" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%A4%AD%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%B8%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%95%E0%A4%B0%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%9A%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%B0%E0%A5%8D%E0%A4%AF.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bhāskara II, commonly called Bhāskarāchārya, belonged to a Deśastha Brahmin family of court scholars, mostly astronomer-astrologers, in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Verses in his main work place his birth in 1114 at Vijjadavida, in the Sahyadri ranges of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a>. Scholars disagree about the exact location; many place it near Patan in Chalisgaon, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, while others have suggested Beed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> or Basar. His father Maheśvara was a mathematician, astronomer and astrologer who taught him, and he in turn taught his son Lokasamudra. He is said to have headed the astronomical observatory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, the leading centre of mathematical astronomy in medieval India. He died in 1185.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Bhāskara wrote the Siddhānta Śiromaṇi in 1150, at the age of 36. It is an orthodox work of the Brāhmapakṣa school and did not displace the canonical texts of other astronomical traditions. Its two astronomical parts are the Grahagaṇita, on the motions of the planets, eclipses and related phenomena, and the Golādhyāya, on the celestial sphere, the Earth, instruments and the seasons. The Līlāvatī, on arithmetic, and the Bījagaṇita, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>, are sometimes counted as parts of it but were usually copied separately. He also wrote a treatise called Karaṇā Kautūhala.</p><p>The Līlāvatī has 13 chapters and covers interest, progressions, plane and solid geometry, the shadow of the gnomon, indeterminate equations and combinations. It is written in compact verse for memorising, and its problems are addressed playfully to an inquisitive young learner, using images such as swans, bees and peacocks. A popular story that it was named after Bhāskara's widowed daughter was first recorded in 1587 and has no contemporary support. The Bījagaṇita, in twelve chapters, is regarded as the earliest surviving Indian treatise wholly devoted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>. It sets out rules for positive and negative numbers, zero and surds, and methods for linear, quadratic and indeterminate equations.</p><p>His chakravala, or cyclic, method gave the first general solution of equations of the type known in the West as Pell's equation. Bhāskara solved a case that Pierre de Fermat posed as a challenge in 1657, which was not solved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> until the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonhard-euler">Leonhard Euler</a>. In the Grahagaṇita he also gave a result that can be read as a forerunner of the idea that the cosine is the derivative of the sine, though he did not develop the notion of a derivative.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhāskara II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The court supported Sanskrit and early Marathi literature: the astronomer <b>Bhaskara</b>'s grandson ran a school under Seuna patronage, and Hemadri, minister to Mahadeva and Ramachandra, wrote the Chaturvarga Chintamani and is credited with the Hemadpanti temple style.</blockquote><a href="/state/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Seuna Yadava dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=23.18,75.78&amp;year=1160" aria-label="Ujjain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Bhaskara II"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3893 5513h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3893 5513h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=23.18,75.78&amp;year=1160">What was here in 1160? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
